<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Italian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was the unified Italian state proclaimed in 1861 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, lasting until the republic was established in 1946. It built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> through colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The name Italian is also used across the site for Italy, its peoples and its earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, which appear in many other empire profiles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Italy" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The kingdom was proclaimed on 17 March 1861, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was the result of the Risorgimento, the movement that emerged after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 to join the states of the peninsula and free them from foreign control. The radical journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a> in the early 1830s, favoured a unitary republic, while monarchists loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> worked for a united kingdom.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-italian-war-of-independence">Third Italian War of Independence</a> of 1866, fought with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, and in 1870 its troops captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, ending more than a thousand years of papal temporal power. The new lira was introduced by royal decree on 17 July 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1882 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1882">Triple Alliance</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, though the tie with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> stayed formal because Italy wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 1915, and in 1919 it annexed Trieste and Trentino-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">Alto Adige</a>, but it did not receive all the territory promised in the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">London Pact</a>. The liberal era was marked by corruption, government instability and poverty in the south. On 7 May 1898 General Fiorenzo Bava-Beccaris fired on crowds protesting against bread prices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, killing between 82 and 300 people, and in 1900 the anarchist Gaetano Bresci shot King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umberto-i">Umberto I</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> became prime minister in 1922 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> took control of the government. The Lateran Treaties of 1929 reconciled the state with the Roman Catholic Church and gave independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italo-ethiopian-war">Second Italo-Ethiopian War</a> began in 1935, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> in 1939. Italy entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in 1940.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allied-invasion-of-sicily">Allied invasion of Sicily</a> the new government surrendered at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-cassibile">Armistice of Cassibile</a> in September 1943, and German forces occupying the north and centre set up the Italian Social Republic under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>. Anger at the monarchy for backing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> led to a referendum on 2 June 1946, and the monarchy was abolished on 18 June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The kingdom was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> that kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> as its constitution. Executive power belonged to the king, who governed through appointed ministers, but he could no longer keep a government in office against the will of Parliament. Parliament had an appointive Senate and an elective Chamber of Deputies. From 1936 to 1943 the king's title included Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and from 1939 King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. Education was compulsory at primary level under the Casati Act of 1859, but illiteracy was nearly 80 per cent in 1861 and took more than fifty years to halve.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Italy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Italian Empire was the project of the <b>Kingdom of Italy</b>, unified in the Risorgimento, proclaimed in 1861 and completed with the capture of Rome in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state lasted until Garibaldi's Expedition of the Thousand in 1860 and its annexation to the <b>Kingdom of Italy</b> in 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state kept the Sardinian constitution, army, administration and dynasty, and even its regnal numbering, and historians usually treat the <b>Kingdom of Italy</b> as the Kingdom of Sardinia renamed and enlarged.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the encounter of Teano Garibaldi met King Victor Emmanuel and handed him the conquered kingdom; the people voted in a plebiscite to join the Kingdom of Sardinia, and the annexation completed the first phase of Italian unification, the <b>Kingdom of Italy</b> being proclaimed in 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1859–1860 the Romagna rebelled, the papal army was defeated at Castelfidardo, and the Marche and Umbria voted to join the new <b>Kingdom of Italy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After further changes of ruler under Napoleon and Austria, Venice joined the <b>Kingdom of Italy</b> in 1866.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
